---
// Page 08 — /handover-dubai/ · DOOR · Template B
// Format per spec: sequential checklist with timings.
//
// Sibling of the snagging pillar in 02-ARCHITECTURE/sitemap-tree.md, same intent.
import PageShell from '../../layouts/PageShell.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import FaqList from '../../components/FaqList.astro';
import ValuationCrossLink from '../../components/ValuationCrossLink.astro';
import NeedsSource from '../../components/NeedsSource.astro';
import { byPath } from '../../lib/pages.js';

const page = byPath('/handover-dubai/');

const answer =
  'Dubai handover runs in a fixed order: developer notice, snagging inspection, defect ' +
  'rectification, re-inspection, then acceptance, DEWA activation, Ejari registration where ' +
  'the unit is let, and title deed transfer. Accepting the unit before the snag list is ' +
  'cleared is the step that costs owners the most.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the order of handover in Dubai?',
    a:
      'Developer handover notice, then snagging inspection, then defect rectification, then ' +
      're-inspection, then acceptance and keys. Utilities, Ejari and the title deed follow ' +
      'acceptance. Inspecting after acceptance rather than before is the single most common ' +
      'and most expensive reordering of that sequence.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I refuse handover if the unit has defects?',
    a:
      'Your leverage is highest before you sign, which is exactly why the inspection belongs ' +
      'there. What you can always do is document the defects, issue them in writing before ' +
      'accepting, and decline to sign off a list that has not actually been closed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What do I need to activate DEWA?',
    a:
      'DEWA activation follows acceptance and needs the property documentation and the ' +
      'account holder’s identification. Do not schedule the move before the account is live — ' +
      'a unit without power is also a unit you cannot properly test.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need Ejari if I am living in the property myself?',
    a:
      'Ejari registers a tenancy contract, so it applies where the unit is let rather than ' +
      'owner-occupied. If you intend to rent the property out, it is part of the sequence ' +
      'after handover rather than part of handover itself.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does the whole handover process take?',
    a:
      'It depends on how many defects come back and how quickly the developer closes them, ' +
      'which is why the timings below are stated as sequence rather than as a fixed number of ' +
      'days. The controllable part is not accepting until the re-inspection is clean.',
  },
];
---

<PageShell page={page} h1="Dubai handover, step by step" answer={answer} faqs={faqs}>
  <h2>What is the handover sequence?</h2>

  <DataTable
    caption="The Dubai handover sequence, in order"
    columns={['#', 'Step', 'What it involves', 'Published timing']}
    rows={[
      [
        '1',
        'Developer handover notice',
        'The developer notifies you the unit is ready and invites you to inspect',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: notice period stated in standard SPAs]</span>',
      ],
      [
        '2',
        'Snagging inspection',
        'Independent instrumented survey against the defect checklist, before acceptance',
        'Hours on site; report typically follows within days',
      ],
      [
        '3',
        'Defect rectification',
        'The developer closes the listed defects at their cost',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: typical rectification window]</span>',
      ],
      [
        '4',
        'Re-inspection',
        'The original list is checked item by item against the work done',
        'Second site visit, charged separately',
      ],
      [
        '5',
        'Acceptance and keys',
        'You sign for the unit; the defect liability period starts running',
        'Same day',
      ],
      [
        '6',
        'DEWA activation',
        'Utility account opened in the owner’s name and the meter read',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: DEWA published activation time and fees]</span>',
      ],
      [
        '7',
        'Ejari registration',
        'Where the unit is let, the tenancy contract is registered',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: Ejari fee and processing time]</span>',
      ],
      [
        '8',
        'Title deed',
        'Ownership recorded with the Dubai Land Department',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: DLD transfer fee and processing time]</span>',
      ],
    ]}
    source="Dubai handover sequence as practised; timings pending primary sources"
  />

  <p>
    The timings are marked rather than estimated. Every one of them is published by the
    authority concerned, and each belongs in this table as a figure with a date against it:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="DEWA, Ejari and DLD published fees and processing times" />
  </p>

  <h2>Which step actually decides how much handover costs you?</h2>

  <p>
    Step 2, and specifically whether it happens before or after step 5. Inspect before you
    accept and the defects are the developer’s problem, negotiated while you still hold the
    only thing they want, which is your signature. Inspect after you accept and you are making
    a claim against a one-year liability clock that has already started.
  </p>

  <p>
    The inspection costs AED 666–999 for a typical apartment. Set against a property worth AED
    1.3–3.5 million, the decision is not really about the fee — see{' '}
    <a href="/snagging-dubai/cost/">what snagging costs in Dubai</a> for the full breakdown by
    unit size.
  </p>

  <h2>What should you be holding when you take the keys?</h2>

  <ul class="two-col">
    <li>The developer NOC</li>
    <li>The title deed, with the area on it matching the area measured on site</li>
    <li>The snagging report and the re-inspection report</li>
    <li>Written confirmation of the defect liability start date</li>
    <li>Warranties and manuals for installed equipment</li>
    <li>The service charge rate and billing cycle, in writing</li>
    <li>An activated DEWA account with a recorded opening meter reading</li>
    <li>Every key, access card and parking permit, counted against the schedule</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The full inspection list this sits on top of is{' '}
    <a href="/snagging-dubai/checklist/">the Dubai snagging checklist</a>, and what the
    developer remains obliged to fix afterwards is on{' '}
    <a href="/snagging-dubai/developer-defect-liability/">developer defect liability</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What do people get wrong at handover?</h2>

  <p>
    They treat the keys as the goal. The goal is a unit with a clean re-inspection and a
    documented paper trail; the keys arrive either way. The owner who accepts on the first
    visit because the unit “looked fine” has traded a year of free remedial work for a few
    days.
  </p>

  <ValuationCrossLink
    before="If handover has gone badly enough that you are weighing whether to keep the unit at all, "
    anchor="a written valuation of the property"
    after=" is the number that decision should be made against."
  />

  <FaqList faqs={faqs} />
</PageShell>
